from h2 import *
A = dict(id="A", levels=L2, kind="Web page", source="Fenland College careers service", numbered=True, title="Starting out in animal care", body=[
 {"p": "Do you dream of a career that involves working with animals? The wildlife and animal care sector is far bigger than most people realise, with roles for keepers, vets' assistants, conservation officers and educators. Some of the best people in the field started with no experience at all, so please do not rule yourself out."},
 {"p": "The first step is to find out which job might suit you. Visit a local zoo, farm or rescue centre, and ask the staff what an ordinary day involves. Most workers are happy to have a quick chat, and what you learn will be more useful than any brochure."},
 {"p": "You are not on your own. Careers advisers can explain the qualifications that each role requires, and your family and friends may know someone in the industry who would be glad to offer advice. Many colleges also run free taster days, which are well worth attending."},
 {"p": "Employers value practical experience above almost everything else, so volunteer as early as you can. Even a few hours a week at an animal charity shows commitment, and the skills you pick up, such as patience and teamwork, will stay with you. If you are unsure about a placement, give it a go."},
 {"p": "When you start applying, read each advert carefully and tailor your CV to it. Keep your application short, check it for mistakes and always include a covering letter. Do not be put off by rejection, because many successful keepers applied several times before they were offered a post."},
 {"p": "Finally, prepare for interviews. Research the organisation, think of examples that prove you are reliable and practise answering questions aloud. Arrive early, dress smartly and, above all, show that you genuinely care about animals. If you do all that, you're good to go."}])
B = dict(id="B", levels=L2, kind="Magazine article", source="Green Futures magazine", title="Why conservation needs young people", body=[
 {"p": "Behind every successful conservation project is a team of skilled people, yet the sector is struggling to find them. The shortage is not new, but it is growing. A survey of 1,200 wildlife employers found that almost three quarters had been unable to fill at least one vacancy last year, and nearly half said that applicants simply lacked practical experience."},
 {"p": "The consequences are serious. Giant pandas, for example, depend on a network of keepers, researchers and forest rangers who monitor their health, track their numbers and protect the bamboo forests they live in. When posts stay empty, vital work is delayed, and projects that took years to build can quickly lose momentum."},
 {"p": "Young people face real obstacles. Many entry-level jobs are seasonal and poorly paid, and competition for places on the best courses is fierce. Others are put off because they believe that you need a degree before you can even volunteer, which is not true. Some also worry that conservation is \"a vocation rather than a career\", a path that demands long hours and offers little security."},
 {"p": "Employers are keen to change that picture. Conservation is, after all, \"the long game\", and a single breeding programme can take a decade to show results. That is why organisations are offering more apprenticeships, short courses and paid trainee posts than ever before, and why many now accept volunteering as proof of ability."},
 {"box": "**Advice from the Wildlife Skills Council:** Thinking of a career in conservation? Start by volunteering locally. Ask about an apprenticeship rather than assuming you need a degree. Join a short course to build your confidence. And remember: you do not have to choose a path straight away."},
 {"p": "The Council has also launched a mentoring scheme that pairs students with experienced keepers for a year, giving them hands-on training and a reference they can use when they apply for their first post."},
 {"p": "In my view, working with wildlife is the most rewarding job anyone can do, and it deserves far more attention from schools and colleges. If you care about the natural world, there has never been a better time to get involved."}])
C = dict(id="C", levels=L2, kind="Transcript of a speech", source="Careers evening at Fenland College", title="Speech to the college careers evening", body=[
 {"p": "Thank you for inviting me to speak this evening. I have been a keeper for nineteen years, and I can honestly say that I have never had a boring day. Look around this room: some of you could be doing my job in ten years' time."},
 {"p": "I did not start at the top. At seventeen I was a volunteer, cleaning enclosures and carrying buckets of food. I was cold, tired and often muddy, but I was hooked. That first year taught me how little I knew, and I am still learning something new every single day."},
 {"p": "So what does it take? First, you need passion. Without it, you will not survive the early starts and the long winters. Second, you need experience, and the only way to get it is by getting your hands dirty. Third, you need resilience, because things do not always go to plan. Animals are unpredictable, and so is life. I know that sounds like a lot, but nobody here learned it all at once."},
 {"p": "Is it hard work? Of course it is. You will work again and again and again to improve your skills, and you will be asked to do a million different things before lunch. But where else could you start the day weighing a giant panda cub and finish it planning a conservation project overseas?"},
 {"p": "The results speak for themselves. Over the past decade, our breeding programme has helped to protect a dozen species, and the number of children visiting our education centre has doubled. None of that would have happened without a team of dedicated people."},
 {"p": "People often ask me what jobs exist. Apart from keepers like me, we employ nutritionists, vet nurses, education officers and a volunteer coordinator, who has the hardest job of all: keeping people like me in order."},
 {"p": "Last month, a student who started with us as a volunteer was offered a full-time post. She was nervous, as we all are, but she kept turning up and she kept learning. That is all it takes to begin."},
 {"p": "You will find the work exhausting, exhilarating and utterly worthwhile. Not every day will be easy, but every day will matter. So jump in with both feet, apply for the placements and show them what you can do. Thank you."}])

